 gift : the interplay between topology, functions, geometry, and algebra\n\nAuthors:\n\nKenji Ueno,\n\nKōji Shiga,\n\nShigeyuki Morita\n\nSummary:This is the first of three volumes originating from a series of lectures in mathematics for high school students, given by professors of Kyoto University in Japan. The main purpose of the lectures was the show students the beauty of mathematics using material that is accessible to people with little preliminary knowledge. A chapter on topology contains three lectures on the Euler characteristics, vortices created by winds, and curvature of the surface. Three lectures on dimension address the Peano curve, Poincare's approach, and three-dimensional manifolds. The book was originally published in Japanese in 1995 by Iwanami Shoten, Publishers, Tokyo, Japan. There is no subject index. Annotation : 2004 Book News, Inc., Portland, OR (booknews.com)\n\n* * *\n\nPrint Book, English, ©2003-<c2005>\n\nPublisher: American Mathematical Society, Providence, R.I., ©2003-<c2005>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Mathematical Gift, II: The Interplay Between Topology, Functions, Geometry ... - Kenji Ueno, Kōji Shiga, Shigeyuki Morita - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/A_Mathematical_Gift.html?id=9xu_fuIhmnYC)\nciteturn647search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Kenji Ueno, Kōji Shiga, Shigeyuki Morita American Mathematical Soc., 2003 - Mathematics - 128 pages This book brings the beauty and fun of mathematics to the classroom. ... a₁ addition formula angle formula arc length b₁ calculus chord coaxal coefficients complex numbers consider coordinate cos² curve of degree discuss draw a tangent ellipse elliptic functions elliptic integral expression Gauss geometric figures geometric means given graph Hence hyperbola independent variable inner ellipse inscribed k2 sin² Lecture lemniscate lemniscate sine line at infinity mathematicians mathematics obtain outer ellipse P(so P₁ P²(C parameter plane curve plane geometry point at infinity point of intersection point of tangency point of view polynomial Poncelet closure theorem Poncelet theorem power series power series expansion projective geometry projective plane proof prove the Poncelet quadratic equation radical axis ratio real numbers relation represented s₁ sin¯¹ sin³ sine function sinx solutions starting point tan¯¹ tangent line tangent line intersects tangent segment triangle trigonometric functions unit circle world of complex world of real φο ... Volume 2 of A Mathematical Gift: The Interplay Between Topology, Functions, Geometry, and Algebra, Shigeyuki Morita\n\n# A Mathematical Gift, II: The Interplay Between Topology, Functions, Geometry, and Algebra\n\nKenji Ueno, Kōji Shiga, Shigeyuki MoritaAmerican Mathematical Soc., 2003 - Mathematics - 128 pages This book brings the beauty and fun of mathematics to the classroom. It offers serious mathematics in a lively, reader-friendly style. Included are exercises and many figures illustrating the main concepts. The first chapter talks about the theory of trigonometric and elliptic functions. It includes subjects such as power series expansions, addition and multiple-angle formulas, and arithmetic-geometric means. The second chapter discusses various aspects of the Poncelet Closure Theorem. This discussion illustrates to the reader the idea of algebraic geometry as a method of studying geometric properties of figures using algebra as a tool. This is the second volume originating from a series of lectures given by the authors at Kyoto University (Japan). It is suitable for classroom use for high school mathematics teachers and for undergraduate mathematics courses in the sciences and liberal arts. The first volume is available as Volume 19 in the AMS series, \"\"Mathematical World\"\". [Input]\n\n### Selected pages\n\nPage 16\n\nPage 43\n\nPage 44\n\nPage 101\n\nPage 85\n\n### Contents\n\nThe Legacy of Tr